      <div class="container">
        <a class="back-link" href="/">← Back to portfolio</a>

        <h1>Employee Retention Analysis</h1>

        <p class="summary">
          Employee analytics project focused on auditing workforce data and identifying 
          attrition patterns through cleaning, transformation, and visualization.
        </p>

        <p class="tools">Python · Data Cleaning · EDA · Visualization</p>

        <div class="hero-image">
          <img
            src="/projects/employee-retention-analysis.jpg"
            alt="Employee Retention Analysis"
          />
        </div>

        <section class="section">
          <h2>Project Overview</h2>
          <p>
            This project explored employee data to identify patterns related to attrition and retention. 
            The work focused on cleaning and preparing the dataset, analyzing key variables, and visualizing 
            trends that could help explain turnover across roles and employee groups.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="section">
          <h2>What I Worked On</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>Led extensive exploratory data analysis to audit the quality of HR and survey datasets before conducting workforce analysis.</li>
            <li>Identified major data quality issues including missing values, inconsistent survey scales, duplicated categories, 
              and incoherent records across multiple variables.</li>
            <li>Performed large-scale data cleaning including restructuring or removing low-value columns, resolving salary 
              outliers, and normalizing categorical variables.</li>
            <li>Implemented targeted data imputation strategies when appropriate and inferred missing values using related fields.</li>
            <li>Investigated inconsistencies in survey scoring systems (e.g., differing satisfaction scales such as 0–5 vs 1–4) 
              to ensure correct interpretation.</li>
            <li>Developed visual analyses using Matplotlib and Seaborn (scatterplots, boxplots, and comparative visualizations) 
              to explore relationships between attrition, salary, department, and satisfaction metrics.</li>
            <li>Framed the project as a structured HR data audit, evaluating whether available data could reliably explain employee retention patterns.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="section">
          <h2>Key Highlights</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>Comprehensive audit of HR datasets revealing that 75% of columns required intervention due to 
              missing values, inconsistencies, or structural issues.</li>
            <li>Large-scale data cleaning including category normalization, scale standardization, and removal or restructuring of low-value columns.</li>
            <li>Visual analysis exploring relationships between attrition, department structure, salary levels, and employee satisfaction indicators.</li>
            <li>Findings suggested that observed turnover patterns were influenced more by dataset limitations 
              and structural factors than clear dissatisfaction drivers.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

